---
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

// The disclaimer is the reason this nav exists.
//
// It was in the header, labelled as such, on the /about route. It is a legal page
// rather than a destination - read once, if at all - so it belongs down here with
// the rest of the small print, and the header is the shorter for it: Solutions,
// Projects, Search and About, and nothing else. The path is /disclaimer now;
// /about is the page about the blog.
//
// The link and the line both stay. The link is the route to the full page; the
// line under the brand pair is the part a reader needs without going anywhere -
// that the site is personal, that the views are not the employer's, and that
// nothing here is Microsoft's. That is the whole of what a disclaimer in a footer
// is for, and it is cheap enough to say in place rather than only behind a click.
//
// Solutions replaces the Topics link that sat here, which is a swap rather than an
// addition: it is the same job - a plain-text route to the index, for a reader who
// has come to the bottom of a page rather than up to the header menu - pointed at
// the vocabulary that still has an index. There is no topics index any more, and
// no per-topic pages either; see the note on TOPICS in consts.ts.
//
// Search and Projects are not in here. Both are ways into the archive rather than
// small print, so they sit in the header beside the Solutions menu, which is their
// only peer.
const links = [
	{ href: '/solutions', label: 'Solutions' },
	{ href: '/disclaimer', label: 'Disclaimer' },
];

// Resolved at build rather than in the browser, so this is the year of the last
// deploy and not the year the page is read. The site is static and a blog that
// publishes anything at all rebuilds inside twelve months; the alternative is
// shipping a script to correct one line of small print, which is not a trade worth
// making. If the site ever goes a full year quiet, the stale year is the tell.
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

{
	/* One row, three columns, no rule anywhere in it: the brand, the small print and
	   the routes, all on the same horizontal line.

	   The small print has been in two other places and both were wrong in the same
	   way. Under the tagline it read as a third line of the masthead; on a row of its
	   own below everything it read as a footer under the footer. It is neither - it
	   is one of three things this strip says, so it sits beside the other two.

	   justify-between spaces the three rather than a grid sizing them, because they
	   are not columns of equal weight and should not be set as if they were. Each
	   takes the width its text needs and the space falls between them.

	   The footer echoes the header rather than restating it: same name, same tagline,
	   at the footer's sizes. A reader arriving at the bottom of a long entry gets the
	   site's name and what it is for without scrolling back up for it.

	   max-w-5xl and px-5 match SiteHeader and <main> exactly, so all three edges land
	   on the same line. */
}
<footer class="border-rule mt-20 border-t">
	<div
		class="mx-auto flex max-w-5xl flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-4 px-5 py-8"
	>
		{
			/* items-baseline, not items-start. The three columns are different type sizes
			   - a 1rem wordmark against 0.7rem small print and 0.72rem links - so aligning
			   their boxes leaves the shorter two floating somewhere above the name's
			   x-height. Aligning the baselines is what makes them read as one line. They
			   end at different depths, which is fine: the only edge a reader reads across
			   is the top one. */
		}
		{
			/* leading-tight on the name and no margin on the tagline, which is the
			   header's treatment and for the header's reason: the two lines are one
			   brand rather than two facts stacked, so the only space between them is the
			   half-leading each line box carries. The default 1.5 leading hangs a
			   quarter-rem under the name's glyphs and a margin on top of that reads as a
			   gap between two unrelated things.

			   0.7rem rather than the header's 0.6rem. That size is set against a text-xl
			   wordmark; this one sits under text-base, and shrinking it to match the
			   ratio would put it below every other piece of small print on the page. */
		}
		<div>
			<p class="font-display text-text text-base leading-tight font-bold">{SITE.name}</p>
			<p class="text-text-3 text-[0.7rem]">{SITE.tagline}</p>
		</div>
		{
			/* The middle column, and no width on it. At 63 characters it holds one line
			   inside the space the other two leave, and if an edit lengthens it, flex
			   shrinks this one first - it is the only item here with a sentence's worth of
			   text to reflow, where the other two would break badly.

			   self-start and mt-5 put it on the row's second line rather than its first,
			   which is the whole trick here and needs saying, because the obvious version
			   of it does not work. Adding a margin alone to a baseline-aligned item does
			   not move that item down - baseline alignment lands every first baseline on
			   the same line whatever the margins are, so the margin would simply make this
			   the tallest item and push the other two down to meet it. self-start takes it
			   out of the baseline group first; the margin then moves it on its own.

			   1.25rem is the name's line box, derived and not chosen: text-base at 1rem
			   times leading-tight's 1.25. That is the exact distance from the wordmark's
			   baseline to the tagline's, so this line lands on the tagline's. Recompute it
			   if the name's size or leading ever changes.

			   The copyright meets it there because the right-hand column carries no gap
			   between its two lines - see the note on that block below.

			   "not my employer's" is not in this line. "Views are my own" carries the
			   claim on its own and the clause naming an employer is on /disclaimer, under
			   Professional Advice, one click from every page. That split is deliberate:
			   the footer says what a reader needs in passing, the page carries the
			   wording. */
		}
		<p class="text-text-3 mt-5 self-start text-[0.7rem]">
			Personal site. Views are my own. Not affiliated with Microsoft.
		</p>

		{
			/* The routes and the copyright as one right-hand block, ragged right from sm
			   up, so the copyright sits under the links it belongs with rather than as an
			   orphan on a row of its own. Left-aligned below that breakpoint, where the
			   block has wrapped onto its own row and there is no column edge to sit
			   against.

			   No gap between the two lines, where this had 1.5. That gap was the whole of
			   why the copyright sat lower than the tagline opposite it: the two columns'
			   first lines are different heights, but the difference very nearly cancels
			   against the difference in where their first baselines fall, so what was left
			   between the second lines was the gap and almost nothing else. Removing it
			   also makes this block the same shape as the brand block - two stacked line
			   boxes, spaced by their own leading and nothing more. */
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col sm:items-end">
			<nav aria-label="Footer" class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 text-[0.72rem]">
				{
					/* min-h-11 below md, the same 44px floor the rail's rows take and for the
					   same reason: 0.72rem of text with no padding is a target about thirteen
					   pixels tall, which is a thumb's problem rather than a pointer's. It goes
					   at md, where the row collapses back to the height of its text.

					   Baseline alignment survives this. The links are centred in a taller box
					   below md, so the row grows, but the wordmark still sits on their baseline
					   - flex aligns the baselines it is given whatever the boxes around them
					   are doing. */
				}
				{
					links.map((link) => (
						<a
							href={withBase(link.href)}
							class="text-text-2 hover:text-text inline-flex min-h-11 items-center transition-colors md:min-h-0"
						>
							{link.label}
						</a>
					))
				}
			</nav>

			{
				/* tabular-nums for the same reason the solutions index uses it - a year is
				   data, and lining figures are what data is set in. */
			}
			<p class="text-text-3 text-[0.7rem] tabular-nums">&copy; {year} {SITE.author}</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</footer>
